    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male named David with isolated systolic hypertension is started on amlodipine 5 mg daily. After 12 months, he reports bilateral lower-extremity ankle edema. His BP is now 138/70 mmHg. Renal function and albumin are normal. What is the mechanism of CCB-induced peripheral edema, and what is the most appropriate management?', '["CCBs cause allergic angioedema; switch to an ACE inhibitor immediately and administer epinephrine", "Dihydropyridine CCBs cause preferential dilation of precapillary arterioles without proportional dilation of postcapillary venules, creating a hydrostatic pressure gradient that forces fluid into the interstitial space; adding an ACE inhibitor or ARB (which dilates the postcapillary venules) can counterbalance this effect and reduce the edema", "The edema indicates decompensated heart failure caused by the CCB; discontinue amlodipine immediately and start intravenous dobutamine", "This is a normal gravitational effect of aging; no treatment adjustment is needed as long as BP is controlled", "CCB-induced edema is caused by renal sodium retention and should be treated by adding a loop diuretic and restricting sodium to < 500 mg/day"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Dihydropyridine CCB-induced peripheral edema** (amlodipine, nifedipine) is a common dose-dependent side effect affecting 5-30% of patients. The mechanism is **NOT fluid retention** (renal); it is a **hemodynamic redistribution** effect: DHP-CCBs preferentially dilate **precapillary arterioles** without proportional dilation of **postcapillary venules**. This increases the **capillary hydrostatic pressure gradient**, forcing plasma into the interstitial space (dependent edema). Management: (1) **Add an ACE inhibitor or ARB** — these agents dilate the postcapillary venules, restoring the arteriolar-venular balance and reducing the hydrostatic gradient (combination therapy reduces edema by 50-75%), (2) Dose reduction, (3) Switch to a non-DHP CCB or alternative class. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; Sica DA, Clin Ther 2003; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male named Howard with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and stage G3b chronic kidney disease (eGFR 38 mL/min) is started on benazepril for renal protection. At a follow-up visit 8 months later, serum potassium is 4.8 mEq/L (reference: 3.5-5.0) and serum creatinine has risen from 1.4 to 1.2 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate clinical response?', '["Continue the ACEi at the same dose because mild hyperkalemia is always benign in patients with CKD and does not require monitoring", "Discontinue the ACEi permanently and replace it with an aldosterone receptor antagonist (spironolactone) at maximum dose", "Add an oral potassium supplement to the regimen to prevent hypokalemia, as ACE inhibitors typically cause potassium wasting", "Double the ACEi dose to enhance renal potassium excretion through increased tubular flow rates", "ACE inhibitors reduce aldosterone secretion (via decreased angiotensin II), decreasing potassium excretion in the collecting duct; in elderly patients with CKD (reduced GFR and impaired tubular potassium secretion), this frequently causes hyperkalemia; hold the ACEi if potassium > 5.5 or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline, and recheck in 1-2 weeks"]'::jsonb, 4, '**ACE inhibitors and ARBs** are renoprotective in diabetic nephropathy and CKD but carry a significant risk of **hyperkalemia** in older adults, especially those with: (1) **Reduced GFR** (impaired renal potassium excretion), (2) **Diabetes** (hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism/type 4 RTA), (3) **Concurrent potassium-sparing diuretics, NSAIDs, or trimethoprim**. Monitoring protocol: Check **serum potassium and creatinine within 1-2 weeks** of initiation and after dose changes. **Hold the drug if**: potassium > 5.5 mEq/L, or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline (suggests bilateral renal artery stenosis or excessive renal hypoperfusion). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; KDIGO 2021; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old female named Edna with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and stage G3b chronic kidney disease (eGFR 38 mL/min) is started on ramipril for renal protection. At a follow-up visit 12 months later, serum potassium is 5.2 mEq/L (reference: 3.5-5.0) and serum creatinine has risen from 1.4 to 1.8 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate clinical response?', '["Discontinue the ACEi permanently and replace it with an aldosterone receptor antagonist (spironolactone) at maximum dose", "Double the ACEi dose to enhance renal potassium excretion through increased tubular flow rates", "ACE inhibitors reduce aldosterone secretion (via decreased angiotensin II), decreasing potassium excretion in the collecting duct; in elderly patients with CKD (reduced GFR and impaired tubular potassium secretion), this frequently causes hyperkalemia; hold the ACEi if potassium > 5.5 or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline, and recheck in 1-2 weeks", "Continue the ACEi at the same dose because mild hyperkalemia is always benign in patients with CKD and does not require monitoring", "Add an oral potassium supplement to the regimen to prevent hypokalemia, as ACE inhibitors typically cause potassium wasting"]'::jsonb, 2, '**ACE inhibitors and ARBs** are renoprotective in diabetic nephropathy and CKD but carry a significant risk of **hyperkalemia** in older adults, especially those with: (1) **Reduced GFR** (impaired renal potassium excretion), (2) **Diabetes** (hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism/type 4 RTA), (3) **Concurrent potassium-sparing diuretics, NSAIDs, or trimethoprim**. Monitoring protocol: Check **serum potassium and creatinine within 1-2 weeks** of initiation and after dose changes. **Hold the drug if**: potassium > 5.5 mEq/L, or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline (suggests bilateral renal artery stenosis or excessive renal hypoperfusion). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; KDIGO 2021; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named Clarence with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and stage G3b chronic kidney disease (eGFR 38 mL/min) is started on ramipril for renal protection. At a follow-up visit 4 months later, serum potassium is 4.8 mEq/L (reference: 3.5-5.0) and serum creatinine has risen from 1.4 to 1.2 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate clinical response?', '["ACE inhibitors reduce aldosterone secretion (via decreased angiotensin II), decreasing potassium excretion in the collecting duct; in elderly patients with CKD (reduced GFR and impaired tubular potassium secretion), this frequently causes hyperkalemia; hold the ACEi if potassium > 5.5 or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline, and recheck in 1-2 weeks", "Discontinue the ACEi permanently and replace it with an aldosterone receptor antagonist (spironolactone) at maximum dose", "Continue the ACEi at the same dose because mild hyperkalemia is always benign in patients with CKD and does not require monitoring", "Double the ACEi dose to enhance renal potassium excretion through increased tubular flow rates", "Add an oral potassium supplement to the regimen to prevent hypokalemia, as ACE inhibitors typically cause potassium wasting"]'::jsonb, 0, '**ACE inhibitors and ARBs** are renoprotective in diabetic nephropathy and CKD but carry a significant risk of **hyperkalemia** in older adults, especially those with: (1) **Reduced GFR** (impaired renal potassium excretion), (2) **Diabetes** (hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism/type 4 RTA), (3) **Concurrent potassium-sparing diuretics, NSAIDs, or trimethoprim**. Monitoring protocol: Check **serum potassium and creatinine within 1-2 weeks** of initiation and after dose changes. **Hold the drug if**: potassium > 5.5 mEq/L, or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline (suggests bilateral renal artery stenosis or excessive renal hypoperfusion). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; KDIGO 2021; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old female named Louise with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and stage G3b chronic kidney disease (eGFR 38 mL/min) is started on lisinopril for renal protection. At a follow-up visit 8 months later, serum potassium is 5.2 mEq/L (reference: 3.5-5.0) and serum creatinine has risen from 1.4 to 1.8 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate clinical response?', '["ACE inhibitors reduce aldosterone secretion (via decreased angiotensin II), decreasing potassium excretion in the collecting duct; in elderly patients with CKD (reduced GFR and impaired tubular potassium secretion), this frequently causes hyperkalemia; hold the ACEi if potassium > 5.5 or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline, and recheck in 1-2 weeks", "Add an oral potassium supplement to the regimen to prevent hypokalemia, as ACE inhibitors typically cause potassium wasting", "Double the ACEi dose to enhance renal potassium excretion through increased tubular flow rates", "Continue the ACEi at the same dose because mild hyperkalemia is always benign in patients with CKD and does not require monitoring", "Discontinue the ACEi permanently and replace it with an aldosterone receptor antagonist (spironolactone) at maximum dose"]'::jsonb, 0, '**ACE inhibitors and ARBs** are renoprotective in diabetic nephropathy and CKD but carry a significant risk of **hyperkalemia** in older adults, especially those with: (1) **Reduced GFR** (impaired renal potassium excretion), (2) **Diabetes** (hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism/type 4 RTA), (3) **Concurrent potassium-sparing diuretics, NSAIDs, or trimethoprim**. Monitoring protocol: Check **serum potassium and creatinine within 1-2 weeks** of initiation and after dose changes. **Hold the drug if**: potassium > 5.5 mEq/L, or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline (suggests bilateral renal artery stenosis or excessive renal hypoperfusion). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; KDIGO 2021; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old male named George with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and stage G3b chronic kidney disease (eGFR 38 mL/min) is started on enalapril for renal protection. At a follow-up visit 6 months later, serum potassium is 5.0 mEq/L (reference: 3.5-5.0) and serum creatinine has risen from 1.4 to 1.8 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate clinical response?', '["Discontinue the ACEi permanently and replace it with an aldosterone receptor antagonist (spironolactone) at maximum dose", "Double the ACEi dose to enhance renal potassium excretion through increased tubular flow rates", "Continue the ACEi at the same dose because mild hyperkalemia is always benign in patients with CKD and does not require monitoring", "ACE inhibitors reduce aldosterone secretion (via decreased angiotensin II), decreasing potassium excretion in the collecting duct; in elderly patients with CKD (reduced GFR and impaired tubular potassium secretion), this frequently causes hyperkalemia; hold the ACEi if potassium > 5.5 or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline, and recheck in 1-2 weeks", "Add an oral potassium supplement to the regimen to prevent hypokalemia, as ACE inhibitors typically cause potassium wasting"]'::jsonb, 3, '**ACE inhibitors and ARBs** are renoprotective in diabetic nephropathy and CKD but carry a significant risk of **hyperkalemia** in older adults, especially those with: (1) **Reduced GFR** (impaired renal potassium excretion), (2) **Diabetes** (hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism/type 4 RTA), (3) **Concurrent potassium-sparing diuretics, NSAIDs, or trimethoprim**. Monitoring protocol: Check **serum potassium and creatinine within 1-2 weeks** of initiation and after dose changes. **Hold the drug if**: potassium > 5.5 mEq/L, or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline (suggests bilateral renal artery stenosis or excessive renal hypoperfusion). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; KDIGO 2021; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old female named Ruth with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and stage G3b chronic kidney disease (eGFR 38 mL/min) is started on benazepril for renal protection. At a follow-up visit 6 months later, serum potassium is 5.0 mEq/L (reference: 3.5-5.0) and serum creatinine has risen from 1.4 to 2.0 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate clinical response?', '["ACE inhibitors reduce aldosterone secretion (via decreased angiotensin II), decreasing potassium excretion in the collecting duct; in elderly patients with CKD (reduced GFR and impaired tubular potassium secretion), this frequently causes hyperkalemia; hold the ACEi if potassium > 5.5 or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline, and recheck in 1-2 weeks", "Continue the ACEi at the same dose because mild hyperkalemia is always benign in patients with CKD and does not require monitoring", "Add an oral potassium supplement to the regimen to prevent hypokalemia, as ACE inhibitors typically cause potassium wasting", "Discontinue the ACEi permanently and replace it with an aldosterone receptor antagonist (spironolactone) at maximum dose", "Double the ACEi dose to enhance renal potassium excretion through increased tubular flow rates"]'::jsonb, 0, '**ACE inhibitors and ARBs** are renoprotective in diabetic nephropathy and CKD but carry a significant risk of **hyperkalemia** in older adults, especially those with: (1) **Reduced GFR** (impaired renal potassium excretion), (2) **Diabetes** (hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism/type 4 RTA), (3) **Concurrent potassium-sparing diuretics, NSAIDs, or trimethoprim**. Monitoring protocol: Check **serum potassium and creatinine within 1-2 weeks** of initiation and after dose changes. **Hold the drug if**: potassium > 5.5 mEq/L, or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline (suggests bilateral renal artery stenosis or excessive renal hypoperfusion). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; KDIGO 2021; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named John with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and stage G3b chronic kidney disease (eGFR 38 mL/min) is started on enalapril for renal protection. At a follow-up visit 3 months later, serum potassium is 5.2 mEq/L (reference: 3.5-5.0) and serum creatinine has risen from 1.4 to 1.4 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate clinical response?', '["Continue the ACEi at the same dose because mild hyperkalemia is always benign in patients with CKD and does not require monitoring", "ACE inhibitors reduce aldosterone secretion (via decreased angiotensin II), decreasing potassium excretion in the collecting duct; in elderly patients with CKD (reduced GFR and impaired tubular potassium secretion), this frequently causes hyperkalemia; hold the ACEi if potassium > 5.5 or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline, and recheck in 1-2 weeks", "Double the ACEi dose to enhance renal potassium excretion through increased tubular flow rates", "Add an oral potassium supplement to the regimen to prevent hypokalemia, as ACE inhibitors typically cause potassium wasting", "Discontinue the ACEi permanently and replace it with an aldosterone receptor antagonist (spironolactone) at maximum dose"]'::jsonb, 1, '**ACE inhibitors and ARBs** are renoprotective in diabetic nephropathy and CKD but carry a significant risk of **hyperkalemia** in older adults, especially those with: (1) **Reduced GFR** (impaired renal potassium excretion), (2) **Diabetes** (hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism/type 4 RTA), (3) **Concurrent potassium-sparing diuretics, NSAIDs, or trimethoprim**. Monitoring protocol: Check **serum potassium and creatinine within 1-2 weeks** of initiation and after dose changes. **Hold the drug if**: potassium > 5.5 mEq/L, or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline (suggests bilateral renal artery stenosis or excessive renal hypoperfusion). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; KDIGO 2021; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
