    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old male named Paul with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and stage G3b chronic kidney disease (eGFR 38 mL/min) is started on enalapril for renal protection. At a follow-up visit 6 months later, serum potassium is 5.6 mEq/L (reference: 3.5-5.0) and serum creatinine has risen from 1.4 to 1.6 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate clinical response?', '["Discontinue the ACEi permanently and replace it with an aldosterone receptor antagonist (spironolactone) at maximum dose", "Add an oral potassium supplement to the regimen to prevent hypokalemia, as ACE inhibitors typically cause potassium wasting", "ACE inhibitors reduce aldosterone secretion (via decreased angiotensin II), decreasing potassium excretion in the collecting duct; in elderly patients with CKD (reduced GFR and impaired tubular potassium secretion), this frequently causes hyperkalemia; hold the ACEi if potassium > 5.5 or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline, and recheck in 1-2 weeks", "Continue the ACEi at the same dose because mild hyperkalemia is always benign in patients with CKD and does not require monitoring", "Double the ACEi dose to enhance renal potassium excretion through increased tubular flow rates"]'::jsonb, 2, '**ACE inhibitors and ARBs** are renoprotective in diabetic nephropathy and CKD but carry a significant risk of **hyperkalemia** in older adults, especially those with: (1) **Reduced GFR** (impaired renal potassium excretion), (2) **Diabetes** (hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism/type 4 RTA), (3) **Concurrent potassium-sparing diuretics, NSAIDs, or trimethoprim**. Monitoring protocol: Check **serum potassium and creatinine within 1-2 weeks** of initiation and after dose changes. **Hold the drug if**: potassium > 5.5 mEq/L, or creatinine rises > 30% from baseline (suggests bilateral renal artery stenosis or excessive renal hypoperfusion). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; KDIGO 2021; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old male named Richard with isolated systolic hypertension (BP 162/62 mmHg), no heart failure, no coronary artery disease, and no atrial fibrillation is seen in clinic. A colleague recommends starting atenolol as first-line antihypertensive therapy. Which of the following best explains why beta-blockers are NOT recommended as first-line agents for uncomplicated ISH in older adults?', '["Beta-blockers reduce heart rate and prolong diastolic filling time but do NOT reduce central aortic systolic pressure or arterial stiffness as effectively as thiazides, CCBs, or RAAS inhibitors; the LIFE trial and meta-analyses showed inferior stroke prevention compared to other first-line agents, particularly in older adults", "Beta-blockers increase pulse pressure by accelerating heart rate and increasing systolic ejection velocity", "Beta-blockers are only indicated for the treatment of asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease in the elderly", "Beta-blockers are too potent at lowering systolic pressure and invariably cause symptomatic hypotension in all elderly patients", "Beta-blockers are contraindicated in all patients over 65 regardless of comorbidities"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Beta-blockers are NOT recommended as first-line therapy for uncomplicated ISH** in older adults. Key reasons: (1) They reduce heart rate and cardiac output but **do not adequately reduce central aortic systolic pressure or arterial stiffness** (the primary pathology in ISH), (2) The **LIFE trial** (losartan vs. atenolol) showed that atenolol was **inferior to losartan for stroke prevention** despite similar brachial BP reduction, (3) **Meta-analyses** (Lindholm et al., Lancet 2005; Wiysonge et al., Cochrane 2017) confirmed that BBs as first-line monotherapy were associated with **worse stroke outcomes** compared to other first-line agents. BBs remain appropriate when there is a **compelling indication** (e.g., post-MI, HFrEF, rate control in AF). (Ref: LIFE Trial; ACC/AHA 2017; Hazzard''s Ch. 37; Cochrane Review 2017)'),
