    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male named Charles with isolated systolic hypertension (BP 186/68 mmHg), no heart failure, no coronary artery disease, and no atrial fibrillation is seen in clinic. A colleague recommends starting atenolol as first-line antihypertensive therapy. Which of the following best explains why beta-blockers are NOT recommended as first-line agents for uncomplicated ISH in older adults?', '["Beta-blockers are contraindicated in all patients over 65 regardless of comorbidities", "Beta-blockers are too potent at lowering systolic pressure and invariably cause symptomatic hypotension in all elderly patients", "Beta-blockers increase pulse pressure by accelerating heart rate and increasing systolic ejection velocity", "Beta-blockers reduce heart rate and prolong diastolic filling time but do NOT reduce central aortic systolic pressure or arterial stiffness as effectively as thiazides, CCBs, or RAAS inhibitors; the LIFE trial and meta-analyses showed inferior stroke prevention compared to other first-line agents, particularly in older adults", "Beta-blockers are only indicated for the treatment of asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease in the elderly"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Beta-blockers are NOT recommended as first-line therapy for uncomplicated ISH** in older adults. Key reasons: (1) They reduce heart rate and cardiac output but **do not adequately reduce central aortic systolic pressure or arterial stiffness** (the primary pathology in ISH), (2) The **LIFE trial** (losartan vs. atenolol) showed that atenolol was **inferior to losartan for stroke prevention** despite similar brachial BP reduction, (3) **Meta-analyses** (Lindholm et al., Lancet 2005; Wiysonge et al., Cochrane 2017) confirmed that BBs as first-line monotherapy were associated with **worse stroke outcomes** compared to other first-line agents. BBs remain appropriate when there is a **compelling indication** (e.g., post-MI, HFrEF, rate control in AF). (Ref: LIFE Trial; ACC/AHA 2017; Hazzard''s Ch. 37; Cochrane Review 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old female named Mildred has blood pressure of 152/70 mmHg despite adherence to maximum-dose chlorthalidone, nifedipine extended-release, and benazepril. She has no identifiable secondary causes on initial workup. What is the most appropriate next step in managing this patient''s resistant hypertension?', '["Discontinue all current medications and restart with a single beta-blocker as monotherapy", "Add a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist (e.g., eplerenone) as the preferred fourth-line agent; resistant hypertension frequently involves relative aldosterone excess, and the PATHWAY-2 trial demonstrated that spironolactone was the most effective add-on therapy", "Increase the thiazide dose to 10 times the recommended maximum to overcome the resistance", "Refer for bilateral renal denervation as the immediate next step before trying any additional pharmacotherapy", "Add a direct-acting vasodilator (hydralazine) at maximum dose without monitoring for reflex tachycardia"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Resistant hypertension** is defined as BP above goal despite optimal doses of >= 3 antihypertensive agents from different classes (ideally including a diuretic), or BP controlled on >= 4 agents. Before diagnosing true resistance, exclude: (1) **Non-adherence** (most common cause), (2) **White coat effect**, (3) **Secondary causes** (primary aldosteronism, renal artery stenosis, pheochromocytoma, obstructive sleep apnea). The **PATHWAY-2 trial** demonstrated that adding **spironolactone** as a fourth agent was significantly more effective than adding bisoprolol, doxazosin, or placebo. This is because **relative aldosterone excess** (even without frank hyperaldosteronism) contributes to sodium retention and volume expansion in many patients with resistant HTN. (Ref: PATHWAY-2 Trial, Lancet 2015; ACC/AHA 2017; Hazzard''s Ch. 37)'),
