    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named Robert has sustained systolic blood pressure of 172 mmHg. His family asks whether treating hypertension in someone over 80 is truly beneficial. Which of the following landmark trials specifically addressed this question?', '["The SPRINT trial excluded all patients over 75, so no evidence exists for this age group", "The ALLHAT trial, which only enrolled patients aged 55-65 and showed no benefit of any antihypertensive class", "The ACCORD trial, which specifically studied blood pressure targets in octogenarians with type 1 diabetes", "The HYVET (Hypertension in the Very Elderly Trial) randomized adults aged >= 80 with SBP >= 160 mmHg to indapamide (+/- perindopril) vs. placebo and demonstrated a 30% reduction in fatal and non-fatal stroke, 21% reduction in all-cause mortality, and 64% reduction in heart failure", "The Framingham Heart Study, which concluded that hypertension treatment is harmful in all adults over 75"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **HYVET (Hypertension in the Very Elderly Trial, NEJM 2008)** was a landmark randomized controlled trial that specifically enrolled **3,845 adults aged >= 80 years** with sustained SBP >= 160 mmHg. Patients were randomized to **indapamide SR 1.5 mg (+/- perindopril 2-4 mg)** vs. placebo, targeting a BP of 150/80 mmHg. Results: (1) **30% reduction in fatal and non-fatal stroke**, (2) **21% reduction in all-cause mortality**, (3) **64% reduction in heart failure**, (4) **34% reduction in cardiovascular events**. The trial was stopped early due to the magnitude of benefit. HYVET provided definitive evidence that **treating hypertension in the very elderly (>= 80) is beneficial**, settling a longstanding clinical controversy. (Ref: HYVET Trial, NEJM 2008; Hazzard''s Ch. 37; ACC/AHA 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named William has sustained systolic blood pressure of 186 mmHg. His family asks whether treating hypertension in someone over 80 is truly beneficial. Which of the following landmark trials specifically addressed this question?', '["The HYVET (Hypertension in the Very Elderly Trial) randomized adults aged >= 80 with SBP >= 160 mmHg to indapamide (+/- perindopril) vs. placebo and demonstrated a 30% reduction in fatal and non-fatal stroke, 21% reduction in all-cause mortality, and 64% reduction in heart failure", "The ALLHAT trial, which only enrolled patients aged 55-65 and showed no benefit of any antihypertensive class", "The SPRINT trial excluded all patients over 75, so no evidence exists for this age group", "The Framingham Heart Study, which concluded that hypertension treatment is harmful in all adults over 75", "The ACCORD trial, which specifically studied blood pressure targets in octogenarians with type 1 diabetes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **HYVET (Hypertension in the Very Elderly Trial, NEJM 2008)** was a landmark randomized controlled trial that specifically enrolled **3,845 adults aged >= 80 years** with sustained SBP >= 160 mmHg. Patients were randomized to **indapamide SR 1.5 mg (+/- perindopril 2-4 mg)** vs. placebo, targeting a BP of 150/80 mmHg. Results: (1) **30% reduction in fatal and non-fatal stroke**, (2) **21% reduction in all-cause mortality**, (3) **64% reduction in heart failure**, (4) **34% reduction in cardiovascular events**. The trial was stopped early due to the magnitude of benefit. HYVET provided definitive evidence that **treating hypertension in the very elderly (>= 80) is beneficial**, settling a longstanding clinical controversy. (Ref: HYVET Trial, NEJM 2008; Hazzard''s Ch. 37; ACC/AHA 2017)'),
