    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female named Irene has sustained systolic blood pressure of 158 mmHg. Her family asks whether treating hypertension in someone over 80 is truly beneficial. Which of the following landmark trials specifically addressed this question?', '["The HYVET (Hypertension in the Very Elderly Trial) randomized adults aged >= 80 with SBP >= 160 mmHg to indapamide (+/- perindopril) vs. placebo and demonstrated a 30% reduction in fatal and non-fatal stroke, 21% reduction in all-cause mortality, and 64% reduction in heart failure", "The SPRINT trial excluded all patients over 75, so no evidence exists for this age group", "The ACCORD trial, which specifically studied blood pressure targets in octogenarians with type 1 diabetes", "The Framingham Heart Study, which concluded that hypertension treatment is harmful in all adults over 75", "The ALLHAT trial, which only enrolled patients aged 55-65 and showed no benefit of any antihypertensive class"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **HYVET (Hypertension in the Very Elderly Trial, NEJM 2008)** was a landmark randomized controlled trial that specifically enrolled **3,845 adults aged >= 80 years** with sustained SBP >= 160 mmHg. Patients were randomized to **indapamide SR 1.5 mg (+/- perindopril 2-4 mg)** vs. placebo, targeting a BP of 150/80 mmHg. Results: (1) **30% reduction in fatal and non-fatal stroke**, (2) **21% reduction in all-cause mortality**, (3) **64% reduction in heart failure**, (4) **34% reduction in cardiovascular events**. The trial was stopped early due to the magnitude of benefit. HYVET provided definitive evidence that **treating hypertension in the very elderly (>= 80) is beneficial**, settling a longstanding clinical controversy. (Ref: HYVET Trial, NEJM 2008; Hazzard''s Ch. 37; ACC/AHA 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old female named Phyllis presents after a syncopal episode. Supine blood pressure is 175/76 mmHg. After standing for 3 minutes, blood pressure drops to 137/62 mmHg. Which of the following correctly defines orthostatic hypotension and its clinical significance in this patient?', '["Orthostatic hypotension is defined exclusively by symptoms (dizziness) and does not require specific blood pressure criteria", "Orthostatic hypotension is only relevant in patients under 40 with autonomic neuropathy and has no significance in geriatric medicine", "Orthostatic hypotension is defined as a sustained reduction of SBP >= 20 mmHg and/or DBP >= 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing (or head-up tilt >= 60 degrees); it affects 20-30% of community-dwelling older adults and is a major independent risk factor for falls, syncope, stroke, myocardial infarction, and all-cause mortality", "Orthostatic hypotension requires a sustained SBP drop of at least 50 mmHg within 30 seconds of standing to be clinically significant", "A blood pressure drop upon standing is a normal physiological response that requires no evaluation in older adults"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Orthostatic hypotension (OH)** is defined by the **consensus definition (American Autonomic Society/AAN)** as a **sustained reduction in SBP >= 20 mmHg and/or DBP >= 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing** (or head-up tilt to >= 60 degrees). OH affects **20-30% of community-dwelling older adults** and **up to 50-60% of frail nursing home residents**. OH is an **independent risk factor** for: (1) Falls and fractures, (2) Syncope, (3) Cognitive decline, (4) Stroke and MI, (5) All-cause mortality. **Measurement technique**: Patient should be supine for >= 5 minutes, then BP measured at 1 minute and 3 minutes of standing. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37 & 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; AAN Consensus Statement; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
