    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female named Jean presents after a syncopal episode. Supine blood pressure is 165/76 mmHg. After standing for 3 minutes, blood pressure drops to 130/66 mmHg. Which of the following correctly defines orthostatic hypotension and its clinical significance in this patient?', '["Orthostatic hypotension is defined as a sustained reduction of SBP >= 20 mmHg and/or DBP >= 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing (or head-up tilt >= 60 degrees); it affects 20-30% of community-dwelling older adults and is a major independent risk factor for falls, syncope, stroke, myocardial infarction, and all-cause mortality", "A blood pressure drop upon standing is a normal physiological response that requires no evaluation in older adults", "Orthostatic hypotension is defined exclusively by symptoms (dizziness) and does not require specific blood pressure criteria", "Orthostatic hypotension requires a sustained SBP drop of at least 50 mmHg within 30 seconds of standing to be clinically significant", "Orthostatic hypotension is only relevant in patients under 40 with autonomic neuropathy and has no significance in geriatric medicine"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Orthostatic hypotension (OH)** is defined by the **consensus definition (American Autonomic Society/AAN)** as a **sustained reduction in SBP >= 20 mmHg and/or DBP >= 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing** (or head-up tilt to >= 60 degrees). OH affects **20-30% of community-dwelling older adults** and **up to 50-60% of frail nursing home residents**. OH is an **independent risk factor** for: (1) Falls and fractures, (2) Syncope, (3) Cognitive decline, (4) Stroke and MI, (5) All-cause mortality. **Measurement technique**: Patient should be supine for >= 5 minutes, then BP measured at 1 minute and 3 minutes of standing. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37 & 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; AAN Consensus Statement; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Jean has recurrent symptomatic orthostatic hypotension with presyncope and lightheadedness upon standing. She has no diabetes, Parkinson''s disease, or medications known to cause OH. What is the most likely age-related pathophysiological mechanism contributing to her orthostatic hypotension?', '["Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison''s disease) causing mineralocorticoid deficiency and sodium wasting in all older adults", "Age-related increase in total blood volume causing venous pooling exclusively in the pulmonary vasculature upon standing", "Chronic dehydration from mandatory age-related anuria (complete cessation of urine production after age 70)", "Age-related blunting of the arterial baroreceptor reflex arc (carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors), combined with decreased beta-1 adrenergic receptor sensitivity in the heart and reduced total blood volume, leading to impaired compensatory heart rate and vasoconstrictor responses to postural change", "Excessive parasympathetic tone from vagal nerve hyperactivity, causing profound bradycardia and vasodilation upon standing"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The pathophysiology of age-related orthostatic hypotension involves multiple mechanisms: (1) **Baroreceptor blunting**: The carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors become less sensitive with age due to arterial stiffening; they fail to detect the blood pressure drop upon standing and cannot mount an adequate compensatory response. (2) **Decreased beta-1 adrenergic receptor sensitivity**: The heart has a blunted chronotropic (heart rate) response to sympathetic stimulation, limiting the compensatory tachycardia. (3) **Reduced total blood volume**: Older adults have lower plasma volume due to decreased thirst, reduced renal concentrating ability, and diuretic use. (4) **Decreased venous compliance**: Reduced capacity of leg veins to return blood against gravity. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37 & 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old female named Virginia has recurrent symptomatic orthostatic hypotension with presyncope and lightheadedness upon standing. She has no diabetes, Parkinson''s disease, or medications known to cause OH. What is the most likely age-related pathophysiological mechanism contributing to her orthostatic hypotension?', '["Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison''s disease) causing mineralocorticoid deficiency and sodium wasting in all older adults", "Chronic dehydration from mandatory age-related anuria (complete cessation of urine production after age 70)", "Age-related blunting of the arterial baroreceptor reflex arc (carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors), combined with decreased beta-1 adrenergic receptor sensitivity in the heart and reduced total blood volume, leading to impaired compensatory heart rate and vasoconstrictor responses to postural change", "Excessive parasympathetic tone from vagal nerve hyperactivity, causing profound bradycardia and vasodilation upon standing", "Age-related increase in total blood volume causing venous pooling exclusively in the pulmonary vasculature upon standing"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The pathophysiology of age-related orthostatic hypotension involves multiple mechanisms: (1) **Baroreceptor blunting**: The carotid sinus and aortic arch baroreceptors become less sensitive with age due to arterial stiffening; they fail to detect the blood pressure drop upon standing and cannot mount an adequate compensatory response. (2) **Decreased beta-1 adrenergic receptor sensitivity**: The heart has a blunted chronotropic (heart rate) response to sympathetic stimulation, limiting the compensatory tachycardia. (3) **Reduced total blood volume**: Older adults have lower plasma volume due to decreased thirst, reduced renal concentrating ability, and diuretic use. (4) **Decreased venous compliance**: Reduced capacity of leg veins to return blood against gravity. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37 & 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips