rld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old female named Shirley with benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) was started on prazosin 3 months ago. She now presents after sustaining a fall upon standing from bed at night. Supine BP is 142/78 mmHg; standing BP at 3 minutes is 108/62 mmHg. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical explanation?', '["Alpha-blockers only cause orthostatic hypotension in patients under 50 years of age and are hemodynamically safe in older adults", "Alpha-blockers stimulate the parasympathetic nervous system, causing reflex bradycardia and syncope unrelated to blood pressure changes", "Alpha-blockers increase aldosterone secretion, causing excessive fluid retention and paradoxical orthostatic hypertension", "The fall was caused by BPH-related nocturia, not by the medication; alpha-blockers have no hemodynamic effects", "Alpha-1 adrenergic receptor blockers (e.g., doxazosin, prazosin, terazosin) block alpha-1 mediated vascular smooth muscle contraction, impairing the normal sympathetic venoconstriction response to standing and directly causing orthostatic hypotension; they are a leading iatrogenic cause of OH in older men"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Alpha-1 adrenergic blockers** (doxazosin, prazosin, terazosin) are among the **most common iatrogenic causes of orthostatic hypotension** in older adults, particularly older men treated for BPH. Mechanism: Alpha-1 receptors on **vascular smooth muscle** mediate sympathetically driven venoconstriction and arteriolar vasoconstriction upon standing. Blocking these receptors impairs the compensatory vasoconstrictor response, leading to venous pooling, reduced venous return, decreased cardiac output, and a drop in blood pressure upon standing. The ALLHAT trial also showed that doxazosin increased the risk of **heart failure** compared to chlorthalidone. Tamsulosin (a uroselective alpha-1A blocker) carries lower — but not zero — orthostatic risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; ALLHAT Trial; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male named Robert with benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) was started on terazosin 6 months ago. He now presents after sustaining a fall upon standing from bed at night. Supine BP is 142/78 mmHg; standing BP at 3 minutes is 108/62 mmHg. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical explanation?', '["The fall was caused by BPH-related nocturia, not by the medication; alpha-blockers have no hemodynamic effects", "Alpha-blockers increase aldosterone secretion, causing excessive fluid retention and paradoxical orthostatic hypertension", "Alpha-blockers only cause orthostatic hypotension in patients under 50 years of age and are hemodynamically safe in older adults", "Alpha-blockers stimulate the parasympathetic nervous system, causing reflex bradycardia and syncope unrelated to blood pressure changes", "Alpha-1 adrenergic receptor blockers (e.g., doxazosin, prazosin, terazosin) block alpha-1 mediated vascular smooth muscle contraction, impairing the normal sympathetic venoconstriction response to standing and directly causing orthostatic hypotension; they are a leading iatrogenic cause of OH in older men"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Alpha-1 adrenergic blockers** (doxazosin, prazosin, terazosin) are among the **most common iatrogenic causes of orthostatic hypotension** in older adults, particularly older men treated for BPH. Mechanism: Alpha-1 receptors on **vascular smooth muscle** mediate sympathetically driven venoconstriction and arteriolar vasoconstriction upon standing. Blocking these receptors impairs the compensatory vasoconstrictor response, leading to venous pooling, reduced venous return, decreased cardiac output, and a drop in blood pressure upon standing. The ALLHAT trial also showed that doxazosin increased the risk of **heart failure** compared to chlorthalidone. Tamsulosin (a uroselective alpha-1A blocker) carries lower — but not zero — orthostatic risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; ALLHAT Trial; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
