    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female named Frances with benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) was started on terazosin 3 months ago. She now presents after sustaining a fall upon standing from bed at night. Supine BP is 142/78 mmHg; standing BP at 3 minutes is 108/62 mmHg. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical explanation?', '["Alpha-blockers only cause orthostatic hypotension in patients under 50 years of age and are hemodynamically safe in older adults", "Alpha-blockers increase aldosterone secretion, causing excessive fluid retention and paradoxical orthostatic hypertension", "Alpha-1 adrenergic receptor blockers (e.g., doxazosin, prazosin, terazosin) block alpha-1 mediated vascular smooth muscle contraction, impairing the normal sympathetic venoconstriction response to standing and directly causing orthostatic hypotension; they are a leading iatrogenic cause of OH in older men", "The fall was caused by BPH-related nocturia, not by the medication; alpha-blockers have no hemodynamic effects", "Alpha-blockers stimulate the parasympathetic nervous system, causing reflex bradycardia and syncope unrelated to blood pressure changes"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Alpha-1 adrenergic blockers** (doxazosin, prazosin, terazosin) are among the **most common iatrogenic causes of orthostatic hypotension** in older adults, particularly older men treated for BPH. Mechanism: Alpha-1 receptors on **vascular smooth muscle** mediate sympathetically driven venoconstriction and arteriolar vasoconstriction upon standing. Blocking these receptors impairs the compensatory vasoconstrictor response, leading to venous pooling, reduced venous return, decreased cardiac output, and a drop in blood pressure upon standing. The ALLHAT trial also showed that doxazosin increased the risk of **heart failure** compared to chlorthalidone. Tamsulosin (a uroselective alpha-1A blocker) carries lower — but not zero — orthostatic risk. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 37; AGS Beers Criteria 2023; ALLHAT Trial; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male named Kenneth residing in an assisted living facility reports dizziness, lightheadedness, and near-syncope approximately 30-60 minutes after eating large meals. Standing blood pressure before meals is normal. Blood pressure measured 45 minutes after lunch shows a decline of 28 mmHg from the pre-meal baseline. What is the most likely diagnosis and appropriate management?', '["Vasovagal syncope triggered exclusively by emotional distress, not by meals", "Reactive hypoglycemia; prescribe high-dose sulfonylurea therapy to maintain post-meal glucose levels", "Acute mesenteric ischemia; the blood pressure drop indicates bowel infarction requiring emergent surgery", "Post-prandial hypotension (PPH); defined as a drop in SBP >= 20 mmHg within 2 hours of eating a meal; caused by splanchnic blood pooling during digestion with impaired compensatory baroreceptor and sympathetic responses; management includes smaller, more frequent meals, reducing carbohydrate content, avoiding alcohol with meals, and walking after eating", "Dumping syndrome; this only occurs after gastric surgery and is unrelated to age-related cardiovascular changes"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Post-prandial hypotension (PPH)** is defined as a **decline in SBP >= 20 mmHg within 2 hours of eating a meal** (or a fall to < 90 mmHg if pre-meal SBP was >= 100 mmHg). PPH affects **25-40% of institutionalized older adults** and is a major cause of falls, syncope, and cardiovascular events in this population. Pathophysiology: After eating, blood is redistributed to the **splanchnic (mesenteric) vascular bed** to facilitate digestion. In healthy young adults, baroreceptor reflexes compensate by increasing heart rate and peripheral vascular resistance. In older adults, **impaired baroreceptor sensitivity** and **blunted sympathetic responses** prevent adequate compensation. Management: (1) Smaller, more frequent meals, (2) Reduce carbohydrate load (carbs cause the greatest splanchnic vasodilation), (3) Avoid alcohol with meals, (4) Walk after eating, (5) Consider acarbose (slows carbohydrate absorption). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
