    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Evelyn residing in an assisted living facility reports dizziness, lightheadedness, and near-syncope approximately 30-60 minutes after eating large meals. Standing blood pressure before meals is normal. Blood pressure measured 45 minutes after lunch shows a decline of 38 mmHg from the pre-meal baseline. What is the most likely diagnosis and appropriate management?', '["Vasovagal syncope triggered exclusively by emotional distress, not by meals", "Post-prandial hypotension (PPH); defined as a drop in SBP >= 20 mmHg within 2 hours of eating a meal; caused by splanchnic blood pooling during digestion with impaired compensatory baroreceptor and sympathetic responses; management includes smaller, more frequent meals, reducing carbohydrate content, avoiding alcohol with meals, and walking after eating", "Dumping syndrome; this only occurs after gastric surgery and is unrelated to age-related cardiovascular changes", "Acute mesenteric ischemia; the blood pressure drop indicates bowel infarction requiring emergent surgery", "Reactive hypoglycemia; prescribe high-dose sulfonylurea therapy to maintain post-meal glucose levels"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Post-prandial hypotension (PPH)** is defined as a **decline in SBP >= 20 mmHg within 2 hours of eating a meal** (or a fall to < 90 mmHg if pre-meal SBP was >= 100 mmHg). PPH affects **25-40% of institutionalized older adults** and is a major cause of falls, syncope, and cardiovascular events in this population. Pathophysiology: After eating, blood is redistributed to the **splanchnic (mesenteric) vascular bed** to facilitate digestion. In healthy young adults, baroreceptor reflexes compensate by increasing heart rate and peripheral vascular resistance. In older adults, **impaired baroreceptor sensitivity** and **blunted sympathetic responses** prevent adequate compensation. Management: (1) Smaller, more frequent meals, (2) Reduce carbohydrate load (carbs cause the greatest splanchnic vasodilation), (3) Avoid alcohol with meals, (4) Walk after eating, (5) Consider acarbose (slows carbohydrate absorption). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Marie with Parkinson''s disease has supine blood pressure of 182/76 mmHg but experiences symptomatic orthostatic hypotension (SBP drops 28 mmHg upon standing). Treating the supine hypertension worsens the orthostatic symptoms, while treating the OH worsens the supine hypertension. Which of the following management strategies best addresses this clinical paradox?', '["Administer midodrine 10 mg at bedtime to raise overnight blood pressure and prevent nocturnal diuresis", "Ignore both conditions because they cancel each other out and the net cardiovascular risk is zero", "Perform bilateral adrenalectomy to eliminate all catecholamine and aldosterone production", "Aggressively treat the supine hypertension with maximum-dose thiazide and CCB around the clock, accepting worsening orthostatic falls as an unavoidable consequence", "Elevate the head of the bed by 10-20 degrees at night (reverse Trendelenburg position) to reduce nocturnal supine hypertension and natriuresis; administer midodrine only during waking/upright hours (avoid bedtime dosing); and use a short-acting nighttime antihypertensive (e.g., losartan or nitroglycerin patch, removed in the morning) for supine HTN"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **coexistence of supine hypertension and orthostatic hypotension** is a common and challenging paradox in older adults, especially those with autonomic failure (Parkinson''s disease, diabetes, MSA). Management requires a nuanced, time-of-day approach: (1) **Elevate the head of the bed 10-20 degrees** at night — this reduces nocturnal supine hypertension and the resulting pressure natriuresis (overnight urine losses worsen daytime hypovolemia and OH), (2) **Midodrine (alpha-1 agonist)** should be given **only during waking/upright hours** and **never at bedtime** (it will worsen supine HTN), (3) **Short-acting nighttime antihypertensives** (nitroglycerin patch removed in the morning, or short-acting losartan) can control nocturnal supine HTN without worsening morning OH. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; Freeman R, NEJM 2008; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old male named Raymond with Parkinson''s disease has supine blood pressure of 165/62 mmHg but experiences symptomatic orthostatic hypotension (SBP drops 28 mmHg upon standing). Treating the supine hypertension worsens the orthostatic symptoms, while treating the OH worsens the supine hypertension. Which of the following management strategies best addresses this clinical paradox?', '["Elevate the head of the bed by 10-20 degrees at night (reverse Trendelenburg position) to reduce nocturnal supine hypertension and natriuresis; administer midodrine only during waking/upright hours (avoid bedtime dosing); and use a short-acting nighttime antihypertensive (e.g., losartan or nitroglycerin patch, removed in the morning) for supine HTN", "Ignore both conditions because they cancel each other out and the net cardiovascular risk is zero", "Perform bilateral adrenalectomy to eliminate all catecholamine and aldosterone production", "Aggressively treat the supine hypertension with maximum-dose thiazide and CCB around the clock, accepting worsening orthostatic falls as an unavoidable consequence", "Administer midodrine 10 mg at bedtime to raise overnight blood pressure and prevent nocturnal diuresis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **coexistence of supine hypertension and orthostatic hypotension** is a common and challenging paradox in older adults, especially those with autonomic failure (Parkinson''s disease, diabetes, MSA). Management requires a nuanced, time-of-day approach: (1) **Elevate the head of the bed 10-20 degrees** at night — this reduces nocturnal supine hypertension and the resulting pressure natriuresis (overnight urine losses worsen daytime hypovolemia and OH), (2) **Midodrine (alpha-1 agonist)** should be given **only during waking/upright hours** and **never at bedtime** (it will worsen supine HTN), (3) **Short-acting nighttime antihypertensives** (nitroglycerin patch removed in the morning, or short-acting losartan) can control nocturnal supine HTN without worsening morning OH. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; Freeman R, NEJM 2008; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
