ne HTN without worsening morning OH. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; Freeman R, NEJM 2008; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old male named Donald with Parkinson''s disease has supine blood pressure of 158/62 mmHg but experiences symptomatic orthostatic hypotension (SBP drops 30 mmHg upon standing). Treating the supine hypertension worsens the orthostatic symptoms, while treating the OH worsens the supine hypertension. Which of the following management strategies best addresses this clinical paradox?', '["Elevate the head of the bed by 10-20 degrees at night (reverse Trendelenburg position) to reduce nocturnal supine hypertension and natriuresis; administer midodrine only during waking/upright hours (avoid bedtime dosing); and use a short-acting nighttime antihypertensive (e.g., losartan or nitroglycerin patch, removed in the morning) for supine HTN", "Administer midodrine 10 mg at bedtime to raise overnight blood pressure and prevent nocturnal diuresis", "Ignore both conditions because they cancel each other out and the net cardiovascular risk is zero", "Aggressively treat the supine hypertension with maximum-dose thiazide and CCB around the clock, accepting worsening orthostatic falls as an unavoidable consequence", "Perform bilateral adrenalectomy to eliminate all catecholamine and aldosterone production"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **coexistence of supine hypertension and orthostatic hypotension** is a common and challenging paradox in older adults, especially those with autonomic failure (Parkinson''s disease, diabetes, MSA). Management requires a nuanced, time-of-day approach: (1) **Elevate the head of the bed 10-20 degrees** at night — this reduces nocturnal supine hypertension and the resulting pressure natriuresis (overnight urine losses worsen daytime hypovolemia and OH), (2) **Midodrine (alpha-1 agonist)** should be given **only during waking/upright hours** and **never at bedtime** (it will worsen supine HTN), (3) **Short-acting nighttime antihypertensives** (nitroglycerin patch removed in the morning, or short-acting losartan) can control nocturnal supine HT